Task 1: Import ISO Installation Images to Storage Domain
Subtask 1.1: Access the RHV Manager Interface
Open your web browser and navigate to the RHV Manager interface

Log in using the provided credentials:

Username: admin@internal
Password: [provided in lab environment]
Once logged in, you should see the main dashboard with various management options

Subtask 1.2: Navigate to Storage Domains
In the left navigation panel, click on Storage
Select Domains from the submenu
You will see a list of existing storage domains
Look for an ISO domain or create one if it doesn't exist
Subtask 1.3: Create ISO Storage Domain (if needed)
If no ISO domain exists, create one:

Click the New Domain button

Configure the following settings:

Name: ISO_Domain
Domain Function: ISO
Storage Type: NFS (or your preferred type)
Use Host: Select an available RHVH host
Export Path: /var/lib/exports/iso
Click OK to create the domain

Wait for the domain to become Active

Subtask 1.4: Upload ISO Files via Web Interface
Select your ISO storage domain from the list

Click on the Images tab at the bottom of the screen

Click the Upload button

In the upload dialog:

Click Choose File and select your ISO image
Provide a descriptive Alias (e.g., "RHEL8-Installation")
Optionally add a Description
Click OK to start the upload process

Monitor the upload progress in the Events tab

Subtask 1.5: Upload ISO Files via Command Line (Alternative Method)
For larger files or batch uploads, use the command line method:

SSH to your RHV Manager server:
ssh root@rhv-manager.example.com
Navigate to the ISO domain directory:
cd /var/lib/exports/iso
Create a subdirectory for your ISOs:
mkdir -p images/11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
Copy your ISO file to the directory:
cp /path/to/your/rhel-8.5-x86_64-dvd.iso images/11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111/
Set proper ownership and permissions:
chown -R vdsm:kvm images/
chmod -R 755 images/
Refresh the storage domain in the web interface:
Go to Storage > Domains
Select your ISO domain
Click More Actions > Scan Disks
Task 2: Attach Installation Media to a VM for Provisioning
Subtask 2.1: Create or Select a Virtual Machine
Navigate to Compute > Virtual Machines in the left panel
Either select an existing VM or create a new one:
To create a new VM:

Click New

Configure basic settings:

Name: TestVM-ISO
Operating System: Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8.x x64
Instance Type: Medium
Optimized for: Server
Click OK to create the VM

Subtask 2.2: Attach ISO to Virtual Machine
Select your target VM from the list

Click on the VM name to open its details

Navigate to the Disks tab

Click Attach > CD-ROM

In the attachment dialog:

Storage Domain: Select your ISO domain
Image: Choose your uploaded ISO file
Interface: IDE (recommended for installation media)
Bootable: Check this option
Click OK to attach the ISO

Subtask 2.3: Configure Boot Order
With your VM selected, click Edit

Go to the Boot Options tab

Set the boot sequence:

Move CD-ROM to the top of the boot order
Ensure Hard Disk is second
Network (PXE) can be third or disabled
Click OK to save changes

Subtask 2.4: Verify ISO Attachment
In the VM details, check the Disks tab
Confirm your ISO appears as an attached CD-ROM device
Note the device path (typically /dev/sr0 or similar)
Task 3: Verify Media Accessibility from RHVH Hosts
Subtask 3.1: Start the Virtual Machine
Select your VM with the attached ISO
Click the Run button (green play icon)
Wait for the VM to start (status should show Up)
Click Console to open the VM console
Subtask 3.2: Verify Boot from ISO
In the console window, observe the boot process
You should see the installation media boot menu
This confirms the ISO is properly attached and accessible
You can proceed with OS installation or press Ctrl+Alt+Del to reboot
Subtask 3.3: Check ISO Accessibility from RHVH Host
SSH to one of your RHVH hosts:
ssh root@rhvh-host1.example.com
List running VMs and their storage:
vdsm-client Host getVMList
Check mounted storage domains:
df -h | grep iso
Verify ISO domain mount point:
ls -la /rhev/data-center/mnt/*/*/images/
Confirm ISO files are accessible:
find /rhev/data-center -name "*.iso" -type f
Subtask 3.4: Test ISO File Integrity
On the RHVH host, calculate the checksum of your ISO:
sha256sum /path/to/your/iso/file.iso
Compare with the original checksum to ensure file integrity
Check file permissions:
ls -la /path/to/your/iso/file.iso
Expected output should show:

Owner: vdsm
Group: kvm
Permissions: 644 or 755
Subtask 3.5: Monitor Storage Domain Status
Return to the RHV Manager web interface

Navigate to Storage > Domains

Select your ISO domain

Check the General tab for:

Status: Should be Active
Available Space: Should show remaining capacity
Used Space: Should reflect uploaded ISOs
In the Images tab, verify all uploaded ISOs are listed

Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: ISO Upload Fails
Symptoms: Upload process stops or fails with error

Solutions:

Check available space in storage domain:
df -h /var/lib/exports/iso
Verify network connectivity between RHV-M and storage
Check file permissions on storage location
Ensure ISO file is not corrupted
Issue 2: VM Cannot Boot from ISO
Symptoms: VM boots to existing OS or shows "No bootable device"

Solutions:

Verify boot order in VM configuration
Check if ISO is properly attached as CD-ROM
Ensure ISO is bootable installation media
Try detaching and reattaching the ISO
Issue 3: Storage Domain Shows as Inactive
Symptoms: ISO domain status is Inactive or Unknown

Solutions:

Check storage connectivity:
showmount -e storage-server.example.com
Verify mount permissions:
mount | grep iso
Restart storage services:
systemctl restart nfs-server
Activate domain manually in RHV Manager
Verification Commands
Use these commands to verify your lab completion:

Check Storage Domain Status
# From RHV Manager
engine-iso-uploader list
Verify ISO Files
# List all ISOs in domain
find /rhev/data-center -name "*.iso" -exec ls -lh {} \;
Check VM Configuration
# From RHVH host
virsh dumpxml vm-name | grep -A5 -B5 cdrom
Best Practices
Organize ISO Files: Use descriptive names and maintain a catalog of available installation media
Monitor Storage Space: Regularly check ISO domain capacity to prevent space issues
Verify Checksums: Always verify ISO integrity after upload
Clean Up: Remove unused ISO files to free up storage space
Document: Keep records of which ISOs are available for different OS versions
